<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abbasid shadow caliphate</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1261">1261</a>–<a href="/years/1517">1517</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as shadow caliphate</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Abbasid shadow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> was the line of Abbasid caliphs maintained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> from 1261 to 1517, after the Mongol destruction of Baghdad. These caliphs held religious prestige and lent legitimacy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultans but had no real political power. It ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-conquest-of-egypt">Ottoman conquest of Egypt</a> in 1517, and it represents the final, largely symbolic phase of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>.</p></div>
